    "created_at": "2026-07-10T18:08:25Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-08-01T06:29:00Z",
    "body": "On the pull request for this, the case of [Link header in subresouces](https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/11620/changes#r2624295385) was mentioned. Various discussions happened elsewhere but I'm unsure about the status:\n\nhttps://docs.google.com/document/d/1OeqpA9JoCXrgIMpq-ujLuZF1tG9MNF5SA0zTuIFtZis/edit?pli=1&tab=t.0\nhttps://github.com/whatwg/fetch/pull/1409\nhttps://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/8865\n\nI understand Chrome allows Link headers on subresources, Firefox does not, WebKit only allows some cases... and the behavior is completely unspecified in HTML (the PR to try that was abandoned).\n\nOn the one hand, there were security concerns and a concrete CVE found in Chromium, so the Google doc considers options where Link headers on subresources would be completely or partially removed. On the other hand, older comment on issue #8865 and some considerations on the Google doc seems to suggest this use case is relevant, and that might be true for `rel=compression-dictionary` too.\n\nGiven `rel=compression-dictionary` is currently being implemented in WebKit and Firefox, but is not shipped yet, and that Google is pondering whether to unship it I would choose the safest option of not implementing Link `rel=compression-dictionary` header in subresources for now. But I'm curious to hear what browser vendors think? ( cc'ing @jesup and @annevk )\n\nIn any case I plan to write some tentative WPT test to check that `rel=compression-dictionary` trigger no fetches on subresources (which is in agreement or at least does not contradict current spec) so we can verify browsers' behavior. --edit: done at https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/pull/61229 and Firefox is passing all the tests.\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
